    "questions": {
      "type": "sbs-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is this a folder of proposal prompts?",
            "answer": "<p>No. It is one set of files per client that every command reads and writes — the config, the scope, four logs — with 37 commands that turn them into discovery reports, audits, scopes, estimates, proposals, plans, QA reports, review packages, readiness decisions, handoffs, monthly reports and retainer recommendations. The proposal templates are six of 29 templates, and each is filled from the scope and the estimate rather than from thin air.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does it send anything to my clients?",
            "answer": "<p>Never. Every command that produces client-facing text produces a draft, marked as such, for a person to read, edit and send. The communication rules it follows — concise, factual, no blame, no promises the evidence does not support, client actions with dates — are in the reference set and in every command.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it legal advice? Are the proposals contracts?",
            "answer": "<p>No, and no. Proposals describe the work and the price and say so in their last line; scope documents do not replace an agreement; the retainer templates describe services, not terms; the pricing guidance is reasoning with hypothetical figures, not rates or accounting advice. Use your own agreements and your own advisers for the legal and financial side.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does it need from me?",
            "answer": "<p>Claude Code, Python 3.8 or newer for the four small scripts (standard library only), and a client. It assumes you can build the website; it does not assume you have run an engagement before — the modules are written for a first one and the example is a whole engagement to read.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does it handle client passwords and access?",
            "answer": "<p>It does not hold them. Access is granted by each platform's own invitation — collaborator, user, editor — per person, least privilege, removed at offboarding. The access inventory records that access exists or is missing, never a value. The client data policy, the .gitignore template and the validation script enforce it; a credential-shaped string in a project file fails validation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will it invent requirements or budgets to fill gaps?",
            "answer": "<p>No. A requirement has a source — discovery notes, an email, a meeting date — or it is marked TBD with a question and an owner. A budget appears only if the prospect stated one, quoted. A report's number has a source or it says unavailable. Those are operating rules in CLAUDE.md and in every command's business rules.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What platforms does it cover?",
            "answer": "<p>The engagement is platform-neutral; the reference set has workflows for Shopify (collaborator access, unpublished themes, apps, checkout limits, the test order), WordPress (admin per person, staging, plugins, backups, updates) and static/Astro (repository, preview deploys, forms, CI) — and overlays for ten project types from a landing page to a migration to a maintenance engagement.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does it fit with the other SiteBuilderStack products?",
            "answer": "<p>This is the engagement layer; the others are the technical layers it sells and delivers with — the Launch System to build, the SEO and Conversion toolkits for audit engagements, the Operations system to deliver maintenance retainers, the Migration system for migration projects, the Shopify toolkit for catalogue retainers. Each is separate; none is required; the modules say \"if you own it\" where one would help.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is in the example?",
            "answer": "<p>A fictional physiotherapy clinic's website redesign from the enquiry form to the retainer recommendation: intake, qualification, discovery, audit and executive summary, scope, estimate (two options), proposal, onboarding, kickoff summary, access inventory, requirements and traceability, plan, tracker, risk register, scope checks, a change request, a weekly report, QA, a review package and triage, launch readiness and report, handoff, retainer recommendation, estimate review and retrospective. The CSVs are real script input; the status and estimate outputs are reproduced by running the scripts on them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use it for client work and brand the documents?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes: one person, unlimited clients, no expiry, and the client-facing documents you produce may carry your name and brand. You may not resell or redistribute the system, or sell its templates as a competing product.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
